# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_BTI_CREATE */
int64_t bti_create(uint32_t iommu, uint32_t options, uint64_t bti_id,
                   uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_BTI_PIN: bti | options << 32, vmo | addrs_count << 32 */
int64_t bti_pin(uint64_t bti_options, uint64_t vmo_count, uint64_t offset,
                uint64_t size, uint64_t *addrs, uint32_t *pmt);
/* CROI_SYS_BTI_RELEASE_QUARANTINE */
int64_t bti_release_quarantine(uint32_t bti);
/* CROI_SYS_PMT_UNPIN */
int64_t pmt_unpin(uint32_t pmt);
/* CROI_SYS_VMO_CREATE_CONTIGUOUS: alignment_log2 | account << 32 */
int64_t vmo_create_contiguous(uint32_t bti, uint64_t size,
                              uint64_t alignment_log2_account, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_BTI_SET_PROPERTIES */
int64_t bti_set_properties(uint32_t bti,
                           const croi_bti_properties_t *properties);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A BTI (bus transaction initiator) is one device's identity as a source of DMA.
A driver pins the pages of a VMO through it, and gets the addresses the device must use for them.
Behind an IOMMU (iommu(2)) the device has a domain of its own: the addresses are I/O virtual addresses from the BTI's window, and only pinned pages are reachable.
When the last reference to such a BTI goes, the device is blocked, not returned to pass-through.
With no IOMMU (the stub) the addresses are physical.

## bti_create

`bti_create` writes to <out> a handle to a BTI for the device <bti_id> of <iommu>.
<options> must be 0.
The meaning of <bti_id> depends on the unit: on VT-d and AMD-Vi, `bus << 8 | device << 3 | function`, in the unit's scope (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` otherwise); on an SMMUv3 and the RISC-V IOMMU the PCI requester id, which the firmware's tables map to the device (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` if it does not reach the unit); on an SMMUv1 or v2 the stream match pairs, `value | mask << 16` (15 bits each) with a second pair in bits 32 to 62 and bit 63 set, which must be the pairs the platform expects; overlapping a valid entry is `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_BOUND` and none free `CROI_ERR_NO_RESOURCES`; on the stub it is only a name.
The handle has the basic rights but not WAIT, and READ, WRITE, GET_PROPERTY, SET_PROPERTY and MAP.

## bti_pin

`bti_pin` pins [<offset>, <offset> + <size>) of a VMO and writes the device addresses to <addrs> and a handle to a PMT (a pinned memory token) to <pmt>.
The first argument packs the BTI handle (low 32 bits, needs MAP) and the options (high 32); the second packs the VMO handle (low 32, needs MAP, and READ for a device that reads, WRITE for one that writes) and the number of addresses <addrs> holds (high 32).
<offset> and <size> are page aligned and <size> is not 0.
The options are:

: `CROI_BTI_PERM_READ`, `CROI_BTI_PERM_WRITE`, `CROI_BTI_PERM_EXECUTE`
  What the device does.
  READ and WRITE need the same rights on the VMO's handle; EXECUTE needs READ.
: `CROI_BTI_COMPRESS`
  One address for each run of pages that is contiguous for the device (here every page: the minimum contiguity is a page).
: `CROI_BTI_CONTIGUOUS`
  One address for the whole range; the VMO must be contiguous.

The two together, or any other option, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The number of addresses must be exactly the number of pages, or 1 with CONTIGUOUS, else `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
Pages outside the BTI's address width or window are `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`.
The pages stay committed and in place until the token is unpinned.
The PMT handle has INSPECT only: it cannot be duplicated or sent.

## pmt_unpin

`pmt_unpin` unpins the pages of a PMT, takes them away from the device (unmapped, with the IOMMU's caches invalidated) and consumes the handle.
A token already unpinned is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.

Closing a PMT handle with handle_close(2) without unpinning quarantines its pages, because the device may still be writing them.
They stay pinned until `bti_release_quarantine`.
A BTI whose last reference goes with pages quarantined leaves them pinned for good, and logs it.

## bti_release_quarantine

`bti_release_quarantine` unpins and frees every quarantined token of the BTI, which needs WRITE.
Call it only when the device is known to be quiet.

## vmo_create_contiguous

`vmo_create_contiguous` writes to <out> a handle to a VMO of <size> bytes of physically contiguous memory, allocated within the BTI's address width and window, and with the BTI's memory type as its cache policy (coherent: cached; non-coherent: uncached).
The BTI handle needs MAP.
<size> is a nonzero multiple of the page size.
The low 32 bits of the third argument are the alignment as a power of two, at least 12 (0 means 12; less, or 64 and over, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`); the high 32 may be an account handle, with CHARGE, to charge the whole size to, else the calling process's account is charged.
The handle has the rights of a vmo_create(2) handle.
No such memory is `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY`; a contiguous VMO can be pinned with CONTIGUOUS.
The job's policy for new VMOs applies.

## bti_set_properties

`bti_set_properties` narrows the BTI's DMA properties, which the handle needs SET_PROPERTY to do.
object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_BTI_PROPERTIES` reads them.

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t address_bits;  // device addresses are below 2^address_bits (64: any)
  uint32_t memory_type;   // CROI_BTI_MEMORY_COHERENT or _NONCOHERENT
  uint64_t window_base;   // device addresses are in [window_base,
  uint64_t window_size;   //   window_base + window_size); size 0: none set
} croi_bti_properties_t;
```

A driver cannot widen what the device manager gave it: a larger <address_bits> or a window outside the present one is `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.
<address_bits> is from 12 to 64 and <memory_type> one of the two, else `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The memory type changes only while nothing is pinned or quarantined (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`).
Without an IOMMU the window is a check on physical addresses.

